course: dictionary
lesson: 4616
title: Hard words from history
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Egyptians also wrote with hieroglyphs, which nobody could read for over a thousand years until the Rosetta Stone gave the key.» The clues are wrote, read and key: hieroglyphs must be a kind of writing that needs a key to read.
explain: Look for clues that explain a word inside the sentence. «Hieroglyphs were Egyptian writing with hundreds of signs; some stood for sounds, like letters, and some for whole words.» The part after the semicolon tells you what the signs did, so you do not need to guess.
explain: Sometimes a word is used in a joking way. If a friend says your handwriting is a hieroglyph, they mean it is very hard to read, not that it is ancient Egyptian. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence, then swap it in to check.
fact: A hieroglyph is a picture or symbol used for writing.
fact: In ancient Egypt, little drawings of birds, eyes and other things stood for sounds or words.
fact: «Hieroglyphs» were Egyptian writing with hundreds of signs.
fact: The Rosetta Stone, found in 1799, helped people read hieroglyphs again.
fact: Informally, «hieroglyph» can also mean writing that is very hard to read.
exercise: «Hieroglyphs were Egyptian writing with hundreds of signs.» What were hieroglyphs? | Maps of the Nile | Egyptian food | Ancient Egyptian coins | *Pictures and symbols used for writing
exercise: «Nobody could read hieroglyphs for over a thousand years until the Rosetta Stone gave the key.» What did the Rosetta Stone give people? | *The key to reading hieroglyphs | A rest from writing | A new kind of paper | A picture of a bird
exercise: Mum says, «Your writing is a total hieroglyph!» What does she mean? | It has pictures of birds in it | *It is very hard to read | It is very long | It is written in ancient Egyptian
test: «Some hieroglyph signs stood for sounds, like letters, and some for whole words.» What did some hieroglyph signs stand for? | Colours | *Sounds, like letters | Days of the week | Numbers only
test: In 1822, who used the Rosetta Stone to read hieroglyphs again? | *Jean-François Champollion | Nobody, because it was impossible | Sumerian scribes | Greek children at school
exam: Which sentence uses hieroglyph in the joking, informal meaning? | «The Rosetta Stone is in a museum.» | «Hieroglyphs were pictures used for writing.» | «Egypt is a country in Africa.» | *«Your homework is a hieroglyph, I can't read a word.»
